# Mutation plan for the login vertical — `POST /api/v4/users/login` and
# `POST /api/v4/users/login/type`.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/login.plan
#
# Five populations, and each is here because a *different* kind of reader error is plausible in
# it.
#
# **The mask** (5). Twelve ids pass and everything else becomes one of four strings chosen by
# seven booleans. Only one of the four is reachable on a live stack, so three of these run against
# the unit tests, which build the configurations the stack cannot have. `mask-saml-flag-dropped`
# and `mask-openid-flag-dropped` are the ends of the five-flag disjunction: on a stock server all
# five are `false` and agree, so nothing but a per-flag test separates them.
#
# **The forwards** (4 + 2 in the app half). Every one of these is an *inversion* rather than a
# deletion, because deleting a forward this stack never takes is invisible — inverting it makes
# the forward fire on every request and the whole parity suite go quiet.
#
# **The response** (6). Cookies, the trailing newline, the `login/type` gate. The two cookie
# mutations that are not about values are about `HttpOnly` and the `Max-Age` source, both of which
# a status-code assertion cannot see.
#
# **The app and store layer** (16). Check order in the preflight, the two lockout boundaries, the
# refund condition, and the two writes `DoLogin` leaves in `Users`.
#
# **The user-agent parser** (10). The ported half of `uasurfer`, against its 55-row Go oracle.
# Two are boundary mutations the corpus could not see until three rows were added for them
# (`ua-safari-version-bump-boundary`, `ua-windows-10-arm-reads-the-minor`).
#
# The two controls are a binding rename in `mask_login_error` and a reorder of two independent
# SELECT columns in `GetForLogin`. If either comes back CAUGHT the harness is deciding verdicts on
# something other than the mutation and the whole batch is void.
#
# **First run: 49 run, 39 caught, 6 survived, 2 controls survived, 2 harness faults.** Both faults
# were malformed mutations rather than verdicts — `explicit-id-falls-through-to-the-login-id`
# spelled the fall-through as a recursive call to the `async fn` it was mutating (E0733), and
# `update-last-login-does-not-stamp-update-at` wrote arithmetic sqlx could not type. Both are
# rewritten above and now CAUGHT. After the repairs and a ten-line re-run the tally is
# **49 run, 46 caught, 1 survived, 2 controls survived, 0 harness faults**.
#
# Five of the six survivors were fixture gaps and each is worth keeping written down:
#
#   - `cookie-gate-accepts-any-requested-with` survived because no request in the suite had ever
#     sent `X-Requested-With` with a value **other** than `XMLHttpRequest`. Absent and "fetch"
#     reach the same place through an equality test *and* through a presence test, so only a
#     third input separates them. `a_successful_login_answers_the_same_user_and_a_token` now sends
#     three.
#   - `body-loses-its-trailing-newline` survived because the success bodies were compared as
#     parsed JSON, which cannot see a trailing byte at all. [D-086] again, from the other
#     direction: the newline is now asserted on the raw bytes of both servers.
#   - `login-type-gate-needs-only-one-flag` survived because both flags are `false` on this stack,
#     where `&&` and `||` agree. The gate moved into `login_type_is_forwarded` so a unit test can
#     build the two configurations the stack cannot have.
#   - `mfa-probe-ignores-mfa-active` survived because `EnableMultifactorAuthentication` is off
#     here, so the probe returns before the predicate is read. `crates/mm-app/tests/db_login_mfa_probe.rs`
#     builds a `Config` with it on and a planted `MfaActive` row, and asserts the counter is
#     untouched on every arm — which is also the test that proves the forward happens *before*
#     any write.
#   - `ua-eval-os-index-fixup-dropped` survived because the corpus row it was aimed at
#     (`)Windows NT 10.0(`) reaches the same answer either way: `evalOS`'s default chain re-reads
#     the whole string. `)x(Windows Phone 8.0; Trident/5.0` is decided by `agentPlatform` alone
#     and moves from Windows Phone to a bare Windows without the fix-up.
#
# The **one** genuine survivor is `mfa-probe-blank-password-guard-dropped`, and it stays one.
# Dropping `!password.is_empty() &&` only changes an answer on a server with MFA enabled, where a
# body with no password would be forwarded instead of answering `blank_pwd`. This stack has MFA
# off for every suite in the binary and turning it on is a server-wide configuration change. The
# ordering it protects is covered structurally instead — the probe is a pure read, asserted by
# `db_login_mfa_probe.rs`, and it is textually before the first write.
#
